course: dictionary
lesson: 10939
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word wade can mean three different things, and the sentence tells you which one. Read the words around it first. «Long legs let cranes wade in marshes and tall grass.» The marsh and the tall grass are the clues: here wade means to walk through water or mud.
explain: Wade can also be followed by through. «She had to wade through a pile of homework before she could play.» Nobody walks through homework, so wade through must mean to deal slowly with hard, boring work. Always test your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have found it.
explain: Wade in means to join something firmly, like a fight or an argument. Be careful with water, though. «If cut off, call the coastguard rather than try to wade.» Fast water, even at knee height, can knock you over, so do not wade out to a tide that is coming in.
fact: Wade means to walk through water or mud, where it is hard to move.
fact: «Wade through» means to deal slowly with boring or difficult work, such as a pile of homework.
fact: «Wade in» means to join something firmly, like a fight or an argument.
fact: Wading birds like herons and cranes have long legs for walking through shallow water.
fact: Fast water, even at knee height, can knock you over, so check the depth and the current before you wade.
exercise: «Long legs let cranes wade in marshes and tall grass.» What does wade mean here? | *To walk through water or mud where it is hard to move | To sing loudly in a group | To sleep in a nest | To fly high above the trees
exercise: «She had to wade through a pile of homework before she could play.» What does wade through mean? | To jump over a puddle quickly | To swim across a lake | *To deal slowly with hard, boring work | To leave the room in a hurry
exercise: «On some flat beaches the sea comes back in faster than you can walk; if cut off, call the coastguard rather than try to wade.» Why should you not try to wade here? | The sea is always warm | *Fast-moving water could knock you over | Coastguards like to swim alone | The sand is too soft to see
test: «Close by, a tapir wades into a pond with its short trunk.» What is the best meaning of wades? | *Walks into the water, where it is hard to move | Climbs a tree | Runs away fast | Makes a loud noise
test: Ravi said he would wade in and help settle the argument. What does wade in mean? | To leave the argument quietly | To hide from it | To read a book about it | *To join in firmly
exam: Which sentence uses wade in the sense of walking through water? | *«The heron stood still, ready to wade into the shallow river.» | «She decided to wade in and stop the fight.» | «They spent the afternoon sorting the old files.» | «He had to wade through the tax forms before he could go out.»
